                <h2>Case Background: A $1,500/Day Account Suddenly Banned</h2>
<p>In mid-October, Lao Zhou, performance marketing lead at a utility app company, faced a critical issue: their "main account" that had been running for three months at $1,500 daily spend was suddenly suspended by the platform. To make things worse, his team was managing 12 accounts simultaneously with budget allocation based purely on intuition—"whichever seems to perform better gets more budget."</p>
<p>The ban reason quickly surfaced: the account had used a batch of ad creative variants previously flagged by the platform, and reusing them in the new account triggered association review. Lao Zhou realized then that <strong>managing multiple accounts isn't about "more accounts is better"—it requires scientific account layering</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Analysis: Four Common Illnesses in Multi-Account Teams</h2>
<h3>Illness 1: Account Health Contagion</h3>
<p>Among the team's 12 accounts, two had received warnings for minor violations. Platform rules mean historical violations can affect new accounts' initial ratings. However, the team had no isolation management, causing these two "high-risk accounts" to unknowingly "contaminate" other healthy accounts through shared IP, devices, and creatives.</p>
<p><strong>Judgment criteria</strong>: Check for historical violations, shared resources (IP, devices, creative packages).</p>
<h3>Illness 2: Budget Allocation by Gut Feel</h3>
<p>The team's budget allocation was: main accounts 60%, remaining accounts 40%—no data basis. Result: some potential accounts couldn't gather sufficient data samples due to insufficient initial testing budget.</p>
<p><strong>Judgment criteria</strong>: Accounts consistently spending below expected average by 30%+ without clear optimization plans.</p>
<h3>Illness 3: Creative Repetition, Intra-Team Competition</h3>
<p>All 12 accounts shared the same creative library, testing similar audience targeting. This meant the same user might see identical ads across multiple accounts, wasting impressions and distorting conversion data.</p>
<p><strong>Judgment criteria</strong>: Cross-account creative and user overlap exceeding 15%.</p>
<h3>Illness 4: Permission Chaos, Misoperation Frequency</h3>
<p>All five performance marketers could log into all accounts. Once, a newcomer accidentally paused all schedules on the main account, causing ~$800 in lost conversions that day.</p>
<p><strong>Judgment criteria</strong>: All members having equal permissions across all accounts without role distinction.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Action Plan: Four Steps to Build Account Layering System</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Account Classification, Defining "Three Layers Nine Grades"</h3>
<p>The team reclassified 12 accounts into three layers based on <strong>health status, historical performance, and strategic value</strong>:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Layer</th>
<th>Definition</th>
<th>Count</th>
<th>Management Focus</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Main Layer</strong></td>
<td>Stable conversions, ROAS 20%+ above team average, no violation records</td>
<td>2</td>
<td>Stable budget, strict resource isolation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Testing Layer</strong></td>
<td>Data accumulated but not yet stable, optimization potential exists</td>
<td>5</td>
<td>Moderate testing, weekly review</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Exploration Layer</strong></td>
<td>New accounts or accounts with volatile performance</td>
<td>5</td>
<td>Small-budget validation, rapid iteration</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Step 2: Resource Isolation, Cutting the "Transmission Chain"</h3>
<p>Main accounts immediately implemented hard isolation:</p>
<ul>
<li>Dedicated device login with fixed IP, cross-border login prohibited</li>
<li>Creatives must undergo secondary original processing, direct reuse from other accounts prohibited</li>
<li>Browser fingerprints and cookies completely cleared before rebuild</li>
</ul>
<p>Testing and exploration layers can share some resources, but must pass "risk assessment checklist" before sharing.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Model-Based Budget Allocation</h3>
<p>Based on industry benchmarks and team historical data, a dynamic allocation ratio was established:</p>
<pre><code>Main Layer: 60-70% budget, ensuring stability
Testing Layer: 20-30% budget, adjusted weekly based on previous week's CPA
Exploration Layer: 5-10% budget, max $200 daily per account
</code></pre>
<p>Monthly reviews recalibrate allocations—<strong>accounts underperforming for two consecutive weeks are automatically downgraded</strong>.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Permission Stratification, Dedicated Responsibilities</h3>
<p>The team implemented "Account Owner System":</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Owner</strong>: Full responsibility for account performance, can execute all operations</li>
<li><strong>Operator</strong>: Can only adjust budget and pause/enable ads within Owner-authorized scope</li>
<li><strong>Viewer</strong>: Data viewing only, no operations</li>
</ul>
<p>New hires receive Viewer permissions only in their first week, progressively upgraded after passing assessments.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Results: Changes After Three Months</h2>
<p>After three months of layered management, the team delivered these results:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Main account ban risk reduced by 70%</strong>: Strict resource isolation made violation association history</li>
<li><strong>2 "quasi-main" accounts emerged from testing layer</strong>: Previously underestimated accounts validated their true potential with adequate budget</li>
<li><strong>Overall ROAS improved by 23%</strong>: Creative repetition eliminated, cross-account competition vanished</li>
<li><strong>Zero misoperation incidents</strong>: Permission stratification prevented individual errors from affecting main accounts</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Actionable Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Health check before new account launch</strong>: Confirm IP, device, and creatives have no violation risks</li>
<li><strong>Don't let "experience" decide budget</strong>: Build data-driven allocation model, calibrate regularly</li>
<li><strong>Layering isn't fixed</strong>: Monthly reviews, dynamic adjustments, maintain fluidity</li>
<li><strong>Permissions are the most overlooked defense</strong>: Establish permission awareness from day one</li>
</ol>
<p>The core challenge of multi-account advertising isn't "account quantity"—it's <strong>controllability of account quality</strong>. Account layering essentially gives each layer a clear mission, enabling resources to work where they matter most.</p>
